.TH RFM 1 "2026-11-24" "rfm 0.1.15" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rfm \- lightweight terminal file manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rfm
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rfm
is a panel-based console file manager for unix-like systems.
It shows one or more file panels (side by side or stacked), a command line
at the bottom of the screen and a status line.
.PP
Typing goes straight into the command line;
.B Enter
runs the command with
.B $SHELL \-c
on the real shell screen.
The panels run on the terminal's alternate screen buffer, so the shell
screen and its scrollback stay exactly as they were before
.B rfm
started.
Before the command output,
.B rfm
echoes its prompt and the command (for example
.IR "user@host $ ls \-la" ).
.PP
A panel can show a local directory, the contents of an archive (zip, tar,
7z, rar, ... \(em listed with commands from the configuration) or a remote
location: SFTP (via the OpenSSH ControlMaster), FTP (via
.BR curl )
and HTTP(S) autoindex pages (read-only).
Files can be copied and moved between local and remote panels, archives and HTTP locations are read-only.
.PP
.B rfm
has a built-in file viewer (lazy reading, hex dump, wrap, text search, JSON/XML prettify) and a simple built-in editor.
External programs can be configured instead.
.PP
There are no function-key shortcuts; all actions use Ctrl keys and the
.B Ctrl+x
prefix, in the style of the shell and Emacs.
.SH OPTIONS
.B rfm
takes no command-line options.
.SH KEYS
In the key names below
.B c\-x
means
.BR Ctrl+x ,
and
.B c\-x n
means
.B Ctrl+x
followed by
.BR n .
The full list is available inside the program with
.BR "c\-x h" .
.SS Panels
.TP 25
.B Tab
Switch to the next panel.
.TP
.B Up, Down
Move the file cursor (arrows never edit the command line).
.TP
.B Home, End, PgUp, PgDn
Start / end of the list, 10 entries up / down.
.TP
.B Enter
With an empty command line: enter a directory, run an executable file as
.IR ./name ,
open an archive as a directory.
Otherwise run the typed command.
.TP
.B c\-t
Mark the file under the cursor.
.TP
.B Shift+Up, Shift+Down
Mark the file and move the cursor.
.TP
.B + \fR/\fP \-
Select / unselect entries by a glob mask (only with an empty command line).
.TP
.B c\-s
Incremental search in the file list.
.TP
.B c\-r
Reload the directory.
.TP
.B c\-o
Show the shell screen; any key returns.
.TP
.B c\-l
Redraw the screen.
.SS Command line
.TP 25
.B c\-a, c\-e
Start / end of line.
.TP
.B c\-b, c\-f
Character back / forward.
.TP
.B c\-u, c\-k
Kill to start / end of line.
.TP
.B c\-w
Delete the word before the cursor.
.TP
.B Esc
Clear the command line.
.TP
.B c\-v
Insert the name under the file cursor, single-quoted.
.TP
.B c\-p, c\-n
Previous / next command from the history.
.TP
.B c\-g
Search the command history.
.TP
.B Shift+Tab
Complete a file name.
.SS Operations (c\-x)
Copy, move and delete apply to the marked files, or to the file under the
cursor when nothing is marked.
.TP 25
.B c\-x n
Create a directory.
.TP
.B c\-x c, c\-x m
Copy / move.
The destination defaults to the directory of the neighbour panel.
.TP
.B c\-x d
Delete.
.TP
.B c\-x v, c\-x V
View the file; the uppercase key uses the whole panel area.
.TP
.B c\-x e, c\-x E
Edit the file; the uppercase key uses the whole panel area.
.TP
.B c\-x s
Sort: then
.B s
size,
.B d
date,
.B n
name,
.B f
directories first.
.TP
.B c\-x p
Panels: then
.B c
create,
.B x
close.
.TP
.B c\-x o
Start an interactive shell in the current directory;
.B exit
or
.B c\-d
returns.
.TP
.B c\-x x
Settings.
.TP
.B c\-x h
Help.
.TP
.B c\-x q
Quit.
.PP
The copy, move and delete dialogs also offer running the operation with
.BR sudo (8)
.RB ( c\-s ).
.SS Viewer
.TP 25
.B Up, Down, Left, Right
Scroll.
.TP
.B Space, PgUp, PgDn
Page down / up.
.TP
.B Home, End
Start / end of file.
.TP
.B d
Toggle hex dump.
.TP
.B w
Toggle line wrap.
.TP
.B p
Prettify JSON/XML (when no search is active).
.TP
.B /
Search; then
.B n
/
.B p
next / previous match.
.TP
.B c\-x h
Viewer help.
.TP
.B q, Esc
Close the viewer.
.SS Editor
The file is edited in memory and written only on save.
.TP 25
.B c\-s, c\-n, c\-p
Search, next / previous match.
.TP
.B c\-r
Search and replace.
.TP
.B c\-y
Delete the current line.
.TP
.B Shift+Up, Shift+Down
Select whole lines;
.B c\-x c
/
.B c\-x m
copy / move them before the cursor line.
.TP
.B c\-x s, c\-x x
Save / save and close.
.TP
.B Esc, c\-x q
Close without saving (asks if the file was modified).
.TP
.B c\-x h
Editor help.
.SS Mouse
Click selects a panel and a file, double click acts like
.BR Enter ,
the wheel scrolls the list, the viewer, the editor and the help windows.
.SH COMMANDS
The
.B cd
command typed in the command line is handled by
.B rfm
itself:
.TP
.B cd \fR[\fIpath\fR]
Change the directory of the active panel; without an argument go to
.IR ~ .
If
.I path
is an archive, open it as a directory.
.TP
.B cd sftp://\fR[\fIuser\fB@\fR]\fIhost\fR[\fB:\fIport\fR]\fB/\fIpath\fR
Open a remote directory over SFTP.
The password and host key questions are asked by
.BR ssh (1)
on the shell screen.
.TP
.B cd ftp://\fR[\fIuser\fB@\fR]\fIhost\fR[\fB:\fIport\fR]\fB/\fIpath\fR
Open an FTP directory; the password is asked once in a dialog.
Without a user name anonymous access is used.
.TP
.B cd http://\fIhost\fB/\fIpath\fR, \fBcd https://\fR...
Browse an HTTP autoindex page (read-only).
.PP
.B ..
at the root of an archive or a remote location closes it and returns to
the directory it was opened from.
Everything else is passed to
.BR $SHELL .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/rfm/config.toml
Configuration (TOML): color themes, archive types, viewer and editor
programs, layout and other settings.
Created with defaults on the first run.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/rfm/panel
Saved panel state, restored on the next start.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/rfm/history
Command history.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RFM_CONFIG
Path to the configuration file.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory of the configuration
.RI ( $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rfm/config.toml ).
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Base directory of the state and history files
.RI ( $XDG_DATA_HOME/rfm/ ).
.TP
.B SHELL
Shell used to run commands and for
.BR "c\-x o" ;
.I /bin/sh
if unset.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR sftp (1),
.BR curl (1),
.BR sudo (8)
